.TH PATCH 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
patch \- apply changes to files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patch
.RB [ \-blNR ]
.RB [ \-c | \-e | \-n | \-u ]
.RI [ \-d " dir" ]
.RI [ \-D " define" ]
.RI [ \-i " patchfile" ]
.RI [ \-o " outfile" ]
.RI [ \-p " num" ]
.RI [ \-r " rejectfile" ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B patch
reads a difference report (a
.RI \(lq diff \(rq
listing) and applies it to
.IR file ,
or to a file named within the difference report itself. All four
difference formats
.B diff
can produce are recognized and applied: normal, unified
.RI ( \-u ),
copied context
.RI ( \-c ),
and
.B ed
scripts
.RI ( \-e ).
When none of
.BR \-c ", " \-e ", " \-n ", or " \-u
is given, the format is detected automatically by scanning the patch
input for a recognizable header.
.PP
For the normal, unified, and context formats, each hunk is located by
first trying the line number it recorded (adjusted by the net effect
of hunks already applied earlier in the same file), then searching
outward from that position through the rest of the file for matching
context; a hunk is never matched against content an earlier hunk in
the same run already consumed. A hunk whose old content cannot be
found anywhere is compared against its own new content at the expected
location: if that already matches, the hunk is treated as already
applied (see
.B \-N
below); otherwise it is written to the reject file. An
.B ed
script carries no context at all, so its commands are applied directly
by line number with no searching.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Before modifying a file in place, save a copy of its original content
as
.IR file .orig.
Has no effect when
.B \-o
is also given, since the target file is never modified in that case.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-e ", " \-n ", " \-u
Force the patch input to be interpreted as copied context, ed script,
normal, or unified format, respectively, instead of auto-detecting
it. Giving more than one of these is a usage error.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Change to
.I dir
before applying the patch. The patch input itself (from
.B \-i
or standard input) is fully read before changing directory, so its
own pathname resolves relative to the original working directory;
every other pathname (the
.I file
operand,
.BR \-o ", " \-r ,
and any filename recorded in the patch) resolves relative to
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI \-D " define"
Instead of unconditionally applying each hunk, wrap the result in
C preprocessor conditionals on the name
.IR define :
a hunk that is a pure addition is wrapped in
\(lq#ifdef \fIdefine\fR\(rq \(lq#endif\(rq,
a pure deletion in
\(lq#ifndef \fIdefine\fR\(rq \(lq#endif\(rq,
and a hunk that both deletes and adds is wrapped so the old text is
kept under
\(lq#ifndef \fIdefine\fR\(rq
and the new text is added under
\(lq#else\(rq.
Refused together with
.B \-e
(an ed script never records the text it deletes, so there is no old
text available for the
.B #ifndef
branch).
.TP
.BI \-i " patchfile"
Read the patch from
.I patchfile
instead of standard input.
.TP
.B \-l
When matching a hunk's context or old-content lines against the
target file, treat any run of blank characters in the patch as
matching any run of blank characters in the file. This affects
matching only: every byte actually written to the output \(em
context lines, and old-line text wrapped by
.B \-D
\(em is always copied from the target file's own literal bytes, never
rewritten to the patch's own whitespace.
.TP
.B \-N
Treat a hunk whose new content is already present at the expected
location as a successful no-op instead of rejecting it. Without
.BR \-N ,
an already-applied hunk is rejected exactly like a genuine mismatch
(written to the reject file, exit status 1).
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the patched result to
.I outfile
instead of modifying the target file in place; the target file itself
is never modified in this case, and
.B \-b
has no effect. If the patch input contains hunks for more than one
file and an explicit
.I file
operand is also given, every hunk from every section is applied, in
stream order, to that one operand file as a single combined pass.
.TP
.BI \-p " num"
Strip the leading
.I num
slash-separated components from each pathname recorded in the patch
before using it to select the target file. Stripping more components
than a pathname has leaves just its basename.
.TP
.B \-R
Assume the patch was generated with the old and new files swapped,
and apply it in reverse. Refused together with
.B \-e
for the same reason as
.BR \-D .
.TP
.BI \-r " rejectfile"
Write rejected hunks to
.I rejectfile
instead of the default,
.IR outfile .rej
(or
.IR file .rej
when modifying in place).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The file to patch. Required when the patch is in normal or
.B ed
format, since neither carries a filename of its own. When the patch is
in context or unified format and no
.I file
operand is given, the target file is chosen from the patch's own
recorded old/new filenames: the old name is used if a file by that
name already exists (after any
.B \-p
stripping), otherwise the new name is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every hunk in the patch was applied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
One or more hunks could not be applied and were written to the reject
file.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error or an I/O error occurred (the patch input could not be
read or parsed, a file could not be opened, changed into, read, or
written, or an option combination was refused).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR m4 (1)
